Nothing needed from you: both runs that train with the compressor frozen are healthy, and nothing new was launched.

**Why I was woken:** the 300-question early check (seed 42, at 100 training steps) was bumped off the shared GPU partition after 2 minutes. It went back into the queue on its own. This is a known, harmless event. The monitor reported it as a cancelled job, so I filed a bug report against the monitor.

**Running now** (compressor frozen; the connector and the decoder adapter train):

| run | progress | held-out loss ↓ (64 rows) | expected end |
|---|---|---|---|
| seed 42 | 73% of one pass over the 28k training rows | .459 / .449 / .437 at 23 / 46 / 69% | ~10:35 |
| seed 43 | 23% | .464 at 23% | ~16:55 (time limit 17:49) |

- **Early hint, not a result:** seed 42's held-out loss follows the run where the compressor and connector were both frozen (.455 / .444 / .438), not the normal run where everything trains (.513 / .455 / .393). That fits the prediction that freezing only the compressor fails the same way full freezing did. Only the answer-quality scores from the judge model (an LLM that grades each answer) will decide it.
- **Watch:** seed 43 runs slower (96 s per step against 71 for seed 42). Above about 105 s per step it would hit its time limit after the last saved checkpoint (step 400 of 437). I would then restart the remaining steps from that checkpoint.
- The eval and model code is unchanged, so the earlier scores of the normal runs still pair with the new ones.

**Next:** I wake when seed 42 ends, or in 4 hours at the latest. Its four question-answering evals and its weight-change check then start by themselves. I will then launch the 20-step test run with only the connector frozen in the freed training slot. That test is waiting because only two training runs may run at once, and both slots are in use.

**Still pending, not blocking:** one of your chats should commit the split-freeze setting in `pisco/model.py` and `test_freeze.py`. Both running jobs use it from the uncommitted working tree.
